ures that are common in econometric studies, such as cross-sectional, time series, and panel datasets. The software also supports time series modeling, enabling users to apply techniques that account for temporal dependencies in the data.\n\nIn addition to regression modeling, SHAZAM includes routines for conducting the Durbin-Watson d test, which is essential for detecting autocorrelation in the residuals of regression models. Autocorrelation can violate the classical linear regression assumptions, leading to inefficient estimates and invalid inference. The Durbin-Watson statistic helps assess the presence of this issue, guiding users in model specification and diagnostics.\n\nSHAZAM also provides tools for identifying outliers, leverage points, and influential observations within the dataset. These diagnostics are crucial for ensuring the robustness of econometric analyses, as outliers can disproportionately affect parameter estimates and lead to misleading conclusions. By incorporating these diagnostic tools, SHAZAM enhances the reliability of the econometric results produced.\n\nFurthermore, SHAZAM is designed to facilitate data management, allowing users to efficiently handle and preprocess their datasets before analysis. This feature is particularly important in econometrics, where data quality and structure can significantly impact the outcomes of statistical tests and models.\n\nThe software's user-friendly interface and comprehensive documentation make it accessible to both novice and experienced econometricians. Its integration of various econometric techniques and statistical tests positions SHAZAM as a valuable resource for conducting rigorous econometric research.\n\nIn summary, SHAZAM is a powerful econometric software package that encompasses a wide range of functionalities for statistical analysis, including regression modeling, time series analysis, and diagnostic testing. Its ability to handle diverse data structures and perform essential econometr